<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Frankfurt</h1><p class="st-meta">1245 CE – 1806 CE · capital Frankfurt am Main</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Main river trading city where German kings were elected and later crowned, famous for its fairs, its Jewish quarter and as birthplace of Goethe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> grew around a Carolingian palace at a ford over the Main. Royal assemblies met there, and its fairs grew into some of the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The citizens gained self-government in the 13th century, and from about 1245 it was treated as an imperial city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A patrician council ruled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> the place of royal elections, and from 1562 coronations also took place in its cathedral. Its autumn fair and book fair drew merchants from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in the walled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-judengasse">Judengasse</a>, attacked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fettmilch-uprising">Fettmilch Uprising</a> of 1614. The city turned Lutheran, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Goethe</a> was born there in 1749.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> lost its freedom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Dalberg</a>, becoming capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a>. In 1815 it became a free city, seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a> and of the 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1866.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-frankfurt.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Frankfurt" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_City_of_Frankfurt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1245 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Frankfurt am Main</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hessian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Frankfurt am Main</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-frankfurt" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Frankfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4879 435 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Frankfurt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5199 691L5229 694M5199 691L5279 723M5199 691L5199 691"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5199 691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-frankfurt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
